<p><strong>Apple Watch heart rate variability (HRV) describes how much the time between your heartbeats changes. It does not measure cortisol.</strong></p>
<p>Cortisol+ uses the HRV it can get, along with other wearable data, to work out a wellness score. Before you read that score, look at the measurement itself: where it came from and when it was taken.</p>

<h2>Where do I find Apple Watch HRV?</h2>
<p><strong>It's in the Health app on your iPhone.</strong></p>
<ol>
  <li>Tap <strong>Search</strong> and look for <strong>Heart Rate Variability</strong>.</li>
  <li>Under <strong>Options</strong>, tap <strong>Show All Data</strong> to see each record.</li>
  <li>Use <strong>Data Sources &amp; Access</strong> to check which devices added data.</li>
</ol>
<p>These steps follow Apple's iOS 26 guide. Labels can be different on other versions. See <a href="https://support.apple.com/guide/iphone/view-your-health-data-iphe3d379c32/26/ios/26">Apple's Health data guide</a>.</p>

<h2>What does SDNN mean?</h2>
<p><strong>SDNN is the HRV measure Apple uses.</strong> It describes how much the gaps between normal heartbeats vary. It's a time, usually shown in milliseconds.</p>
<p><a href="https://developer.apple.com/documentation/healthkit/hkquantitytypeidentifier/heartratevariabilitysdnn">Apple documents the SDNN data type</a> that apps read from Health.</p>
<p>SDNN and RMSSD are worked out in different ways. Another app's score may also use a different recording window, or its own way of reading the data.</p>
<p>So don't treat two numbers as the same just because both say "HRV". Check the method, units and recording conditions first. Our <a href="/hrv/normal-range/">guide to HRV ranges and measurement methods</a> explains why comparing is hard.</p>

<ZenTip image="/app-ui/zen_presenting_new.webp">
  Comparing HRV from two apps or watches? First check that both use the <strong>same measure</strong>, like SDNN.
</ZenTip>

<h2>How often does it update?</h2>
<p><strong>There's no fixed HRV schedule.</strong> Apple says background heart-rate readings happen at times that depend on your activity. HRV comes from the light-based (optical) sensor.</p>
<p>A new heart-rate value doesn't mean there's a new HRV sample. Check the HRV record itself. Read <a href="https://support.apple.com/en-us/120277">Apple's monitoring explanation</a>.</p>
<p>If your app shows an old value, compare it with Health before you assume the Watch stopped recording. Our <a href="/blog/apple-watch-hrv-not-updating/">HRV troubleshooting guide</a> covers times, permissions, fit and power settings.</p>

<h2>How does Cortisol+ use HRV?</h2>
<p><strong>HRV is one input to Cortisol+'s 0–100 wellness score.</strong> The others are sleep, heart-rate and activity data, when available.</p>
<p>The score starts with default settings and can mix in your own history as readings build up. Missing inputs and older records affect how you should read it. The <a href="/how-it-works/">published methodology</a> describes the weights, baseline rules and limits.</p>
<p>The score is not a measured cortisol level or an observed hormone curve. We have not shown its clinical accuracy against lab cortisol testing.</p>
<p>It can't tell you a supplement worked, diagnose a hormone disorder, or explain a symptom from HRV alone.</p>

